openfx-gmic: G'MIC filters as OpenFX plug-ins
==============================================

openfx-gmic makes the filters of G'MIC (https://gmic.eu), an open-source image processing
framework, available as OpenFX plug-ins, mainly for Natron (https://natrongithub.github.io).
A single GMIC.ofx bundle registers one plug-in per filter of the G'MIC standard library and its
community filters: 1201 plug-ins with G'MIC 4.0.8 (815 in COMMERCIAL builds). Changes since
Natron 2.5 are in CHANGELOG.md.

It was written by Tobias Fleischer (reduxFX Productions, https://www.reduxfx.com), and is now
maintained by the Natron developers at https://github.com/NatronGitHub/openfx-gmic.

Licence
-------

Dual-licensed, choose one of (see COPYING):
- CeCILL-C, close to the GNU LGPL (Licence_CeCILL-C_V1-en.txt,
  http://www.cecill.info/licences/Licence_CeCILL-C_V1-en.html);
- CeCILL v2.0, compatible with the GNU GPL (Licence_CeCILL_V2-en.txt,
  http://www.cecill.info/licences/Licence_CeCILL_V2-en.html).

COMMERCIAL builds (-DOFX_LICENSE=COMMERCIAL), made for Natron's COMMERCIAL builds, contain no GPL
code: only code compatible with the CeCILL-C option (pocketfft is BSD-3-Clause, some filters
CeCILL-B). The default (GPL) build also includes community filters under other licences
(CeCILL-only, GPL, non-commercial: see gmic_stdlib_commercial.txt), and GPL builds may link FFTW
(GPL) instead of pocketfft (FFT=fftw3). The licence texts are installed in the bundle's
Contents/Resources. COMMERCIAL builds are no longer CI-tested, and are low priority.

Getting the sources
-------------------

    git clone --recursive https://github.com/NatronGitHub/openfx-gmic.git
    git submodule update -i    # in an existing clone: the openfx and SupportExt (GPL builds) submodules are required

The G'MIC sources (version GMICVER in gmic_sources.txt, 4.0.8) are downloaded at build time and are
not in git: the release tarball gmic_<version>.tar.gz, from the GitHub release of GreycLab/gmic,
gmic.eu or the MacPorts distfiles mirror, checked against GMICSRC_SHA256_<version>. They are then
patched by the scripts that gmic_sources.txt lists and documents (CIMG_PATCHES, GMIC_PATCHES; and
POCKETFFT_PATCHES for pocketfft's header), and
gmic_hotfixes.gmic replaces the definitions of broken filters (each fix is skipped once G'MIC has
it). Checkouts built with the former Makefile may still have its files at the top: CImg.h,
gmic.cpp, gmic.h, gmic_libc.h and gmic_stdlib_community.h (which the CMake build refuses), and
gmic_hotfixes.h, gmic_hotfixes.version, gmic_sources.license, gmic_sources.request,
gmic_stdlib_community.h.commands, Licence_pocketfft.txt and gmic_stdlib_commercial/: delete them.

Requirements
------------

- A C++17 or later compiler (C++20 for Qt6 builds of Natron), pkg-config, and CMake >= 3.21
  (with Ninja for the example below, or any other generator).
- Libraries, found through pkg-config: zlib, libpng; with FFT=fftw3 only,
  fftw3 >= 3.3.5 and fftw3_threads (the default FFT, pocketfft, is header-only and downloaded at
  build time).
- Linux: the static archives of these libraries (libz.a, libpng16.a; libfftw3.a and
  libfftw3_threads.a with FFT=fftw3), built with -fPIC, found through pkg-config. They are linked
  statically, so that GMIC.ofx needs only system libraries. Distributions' archives are often not
  built with -fPIC (e.g. Ubuntu's libpng16.a), and then fail to link: build them with
  .github/workflows/build_static_deps.sh <prefix>, as CI does, and set
  PKG_CONFIG_PATH=<prefix>/lib/pkgconfig. -DGMIC_STATIC_DEPS=OFF links
  the shared zlib and libpng instead: distribution packages and Natron's
  release builds use it (Natron's installer bundles these libraries).
- The OpenGL headers and library (e.g. libgl-dev, mesa-libGL-devel; macOS and Windows have them),
  for the point handles (openfx-supportext, in the SupportExt submodule), except in COMMERCIAL
  builds.
- Python 3, for the render test only.
- Platforms: Linux, macOS (Apple clang; libraries from MacPorts or Homebrew) and Windows: MSVC
  x64 with CMake, as in CI (see "Building with MSVC" below), or MSYS2 (UCRT64, tested weekly;
  libraries from its packages: mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-zlib, -libpng, -libgomp and -pkgconf).

Runtime requirements on Linux: GMIC.ofx needs only the system libraries libc, libm, libstdc++,
libgcc_s, libgomp (OpenMP builds; libgomp is a separate package, which minimal systems may not
have) and libGL (or libOpenGL and libGLX; not in COMMERCIAL builds), plus libpthread, libdl and librt before glibc 2.34. Natron 2.6's Linux baseline is Rocky
Linux 9 (glibc 2.34, GCC 11's libstdc++): a build there runs on newer distributions.

Downloads: the plug-in doesn't use libcurl, on any system. The filters that download data (CLUTs
of Color Presets and Simulate Film, CNN networks, sample images...) run the system's curl command:
/usr/bin/curl on macOS, curl.exe from the system directory (System32, part of Windows 10 1803 and
later; then PowerShell) on Windows, and curl, or wget if curl fails, from PATH on Linux. The
plug-in sets these paths, so that CImg doesn't look for the commands in the host's current
directory first (a PATH that contains "." or an empty entry still does). Without them, these
filters fail with "Unreachable network file". Unlike the libcurl code of earlier builds, the curl
command verifies TLS certificates: downloads may fail behind a proxy that intercepts TLS with a
certificate that the system doesn't trust.

Building with CMake
-------------------

    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    cmake --build build              # build/Plugins/GMIC.ofx.bundle
    cmake --install build --prefix <dir>   # <dir>/OFX/Plugins/GMIC.ofx.bundle

For an Xcode or Visual Studio project, use CMake's generator: cmake -G Xcode, or
cmake -G "Visual Studio <n> <year>" -A x64 (cmake --help lists them). The configurations of
such a project share build/Plugins/GMIC.ofx.bundle: the last one built is the one there.

Distribute the installed bundle (cmake --install), not build/Plugins: the install finishes it
(mode 755; macOS: rpaths into /opt/local, /usr/local and Homebrew removed, re-signed;
OFX_DEPLOY_BUNDLES: bundled libraries).

Options:
- CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE: Release (default), Debug or RelWithDebInfo.
- OFX_CXX_STANDARD: 17 (default, the minimum) or 20.
- OFX_USE_OPENMP: ON (default); OFX_LIBOMP_ROOT: libomp for Apple clang (default:
  MacPorts'; Homebrew: -DOFX_LIBOMP_ROOT=$(brew --prefix libomp)).
- OFX_LICENSE: GPL (default) or COMMERCIAL (see "Licence"; gmic_stdlib_commercial.txt lists the
  filters left out, and why); FFT: pocketfft (default, downloaded; BSD-3-Clause, as in openfx-misc)
  or fftw3 (FFTW, opt-in, GPL builds only).
- GMIC_TARBALL: a downloaded gmic_<version>.tar.gz, for offline builds; GMIC_DOWNLOAD_DIR: where
  to download it (default: the build directory).
- GMIC_POCKETFFT: a downloaded pocketfft_hdronly.h, for offline builds: the
  file at gmic_sources.txt's POCKETFFTCOMMIT, checked against POCKETFFT_SHA256 (downloaded there if
  missing), unpatched (the build patches a copy).
- GMIC_STATIC_DEPS: Linux only, ON (default): link zlib, libpng and FFTW statically; OFF: shared.
- OFX_DEPLOY_BUNDLES=ON: on macOS, the install step copies the libraries into the bundle.
  GMIC.ofx targets macOS 14.0 by default (CMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET, MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET),
  but the copied libraries keep the macOS version they were built for. CI builds, which aren't
  shipped, target the runner's macOS (15 on macos-15), as do the MacPorts libraries they bundle;
  Natron's release builds, built on macOS 14, target 14.0.
- GMIC_BUILD_TESTS=ON: also build openfx's hostDemo, for the render test.
- GMIC_PLUGIN_LIST: ON (default unless cross-compiling without CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING_EMULATOR) or
  OFF: ship the list of the plug-ins, which the build makes by loading the plug-in. Set it OFF
  when the build machine can't load the plug-in but CMake doesn't see it as cross-compiling:
  MSVC ARM64 or Arm64EC built on an x64 machine, or arm64 built on an Intel Mac. Without the
  list, every plug-in is registered.
- GMIC_VCOMP_DLL: MSVC with OpenMP: the vcomp140.dll copied into the bundle (default: the
  redistributable one of the compiler, see below).

Building with MSVC
------------------

CI builds the Windows x64 bundle with Visual Studio's compiler (cl.exe), from a "x64 Native Tools"
environment (vcvars64.bat), with CMake and Ninja, from Git for Windows' bash: the patches of the
G'MIC sources need its sh and awk in PATH (or -DGMIC_PATCH_SH=<path to sh.exe>). zlib and libpng
(and expat, for the test hosts of GMIC_BUILD_TESTS) come from vcpkg, at the versions pinned by
vcpkg.json (its builtin-baseline), as static libraries with the static C runtime:

    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=cl -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=cl -DCMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY=MultiThreaded \
      -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=<vcpkg>/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake -DVCPKG_TARGET_TRIPLET=x64-windows-static
    cmake --build build
    cmake --install build --prefix <dir>

With a Visual Studio generator (Debug and Release in one project), use
-DCMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY='MultiThreaded$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:Debug>' instead.

OpenMP uses MSVC's /openmp (OpenMP 2.0): the bundle ships the compiler's redistributable
vcomp140.dll (VC/Redist/MSVC/<version>/x64/Microsoft.VC<nnn>.OpenMP) next to GMIC.ofx, and a
manifest embedded into GMIC.ofx makes the loader use that copy. GMIC.ofx then imports only
KERNEL32, SHELL32 and VCOMP140 (no C++ runtime, zlib or libpng DLL). The plug-in keeps vcomp140.dll
loaded once it has rendered (unloading it under its idle threads crashed hosts that unload
plug-ins). Debug builds link the same vcomp140.dll (vcomp140d.dll is not redistributable).
Contents/Resources/vcomp140-NOTICE.txt says that vcomp140.dll is Microsoft's redistributable, not
under the plug-in's licence. /openmp:llvm is not supported.

Windows on Arm (CI builds them weekly, natively on windows-11-arm, from an "ARM64 Native Tools"
environment, vcvarsall.bat arm64):
- ARM64: as above, with -DVCPKG_TARGET_TRIPLET=arm64-windows-static. The plug-in is installed
  into Contents/Win-arm64, a reserved folder of the OFX packaging spec, for native ARM64 hosts.
- Arm64EC: also with CFLAGS=-arm64EC CXXFLAGS=-arm64EC in the environment, and
  -DVCPKG_TARGET_TRIPLET=arm64ec-windows-static (vcpkg-triplets/, found through
  vcpkg-configuration.json): Contents/Win-arm64ec, for Arm64EC hosts (and x64 hosts running under
  emulation, which also look there).
- Arm64X, one GMIC.ofx with both, Microsoft's two-pass recipe: an ARM64 build configured with
  -DGMIC_ARM64X=ARM64 -DGMIC_ARM64X_RSP=<file> writes its linker inputs into <file> (MSVC 17.11
  or later); an Arm64EC build configured afterwards with -DGMIC_ARM64X=ARM64EC and the same
  GMIC_ARM64X_RSP links them into an Arm64X GMIC.ofx, installed into Contents/Win-arm64ec.
OpenMP works on both (the ARM64 Redist vcomp140.dll is an Arm64X image, bundled as on x64).
The weekly CI artifact openfx-gmic-windows-msvc is one bundle for x64, Arm64EC and ARM64 hosts:
Contents/Win64 (x64), Contents/Win-arm64ec (the Arm64X GMIC.ofx) and Contents/Win-arm64 (the
ARM64 one), each with its vcomp140.dll. It is only made when both Windows jobs succeed, so after
a failed week there is no fresh Windows download until the next green weekly or on-demand run;
the previous bundle stays available for up to 14 days. The weekly run is never skipped: the
toolchains (package managers, runner images) move even when master doesn't.
Arm64EC with a CMake that doesn't report ARM64EC as the compiler's architecture: -arm64EC (or
/arm64EC) in CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS is enough for the choice of vcomp140.dll.

Filters
-------

The filters come from the first of these that the plug-in can use:
- ~/.config/gmic/gmic_ofx.gmic (Windows: %APPDATA%/gmic/gmic_ofx.gmic), if not empty;
- ~/.config/gmic/gmic_stdlib.gmic (Windows: %APPDATA%/gmic/gmic_stdlib.gmic), if not empty;
- only with OFX_GMIC_UPDATE_FILE=1 (see "Environment"), G'MIC's update file, update408.gmic
  (408: the G'MIC version the plug-in is built with, 4.0.8), if it has filters;
- the standard library built into the plug-in.
A user file (or the update file) replaces the whole filter list. Plug-in identifiers come from
the filter names.
The commands that the file defines are passed to G'MIC on every render, as in G'MIC-Qt, so its
filters can call them; they replace the built-in commands of the same name. A copy of an older
gmic_stdlib.gmic thus runs its own, older commands, not those of the plug-in's G'MIC. The
plug-in's hot fixes for G'MIC bugs still apply to the commands that the file copies unchanged
from the plug-in's G'MIC; a command that the file defines another way (e.g. fixed in a newer
update file) keeps its definition (the plug-in prints a note when it loads: "'<command>' is
defined by <file>: its definition is kept").
After adding, removing or editing a user file, clear the host's plug-in cache (see "Choosing the
plug-ins"): a cached list of plug-ins numbers them as the old filter list did.

As in G'MIC-Qt, the user source ~/.gmic (Windows: %USERPROFILE%\user.gmic), which G'MIC's
command line also loads, is read after these filters (after the file above, or the built-in
ones): its filters are added in their own folders under G'MIC (its folders start again from
the top), and its commands are passed to G'MIC on every render, after those of the file above,
which they replace. It may hold filters, commands
or both. It is read when the host loads the plug-in: after adding, removing or editing it, restart
the host and clear its plug-in cache, as for a user file (new plug-ins may appear, and the others
may be numbered differently).

Filter files are read with G'MIC-Qt's rules: each folder ("#@gui <folder>") is a menu group,
nested in the previous one unless its name starts with "_" (each "_" goes back one level), e.g.
G'MIC/Testing/Samj/Colors; "#@gui_en hide(<folder>/.../<filter>)" removes a filter, or a whole
folder, also one read before it (e.g. hide(Colors/Sepia) in ~/.gmic hides a built-in filter); a
"!" before a filter name (G'MIC-Qt's warning mark) is not part of the name; blanks may come before
"#@gui"; and the preview factor after the preview command may be a decimal number, e.g. (1.5).
Menu groups don't change plug-in identifiers, which come from the filter names.

Filters that can't work in an OpenFX host are hidden only by generic rules on their definitions
(batch converters of image sequences, filters that write files that the user doesn't choose, tools
that wait for keys in a window), never by name: as in G'MIC-Qt, the others are shown. Some of them write
files on every render: e.g. Intarsia writes an HTML page and PNG images into its Output Directory,
which is empty by default, i.e. the home folder, as in G'MIC-Qt (an empty folder parameter is the
home folder), so set it first.

As in G'MIC-Qt, each page of G'MIC's About folder is a plug-in of its own, in the G'MIC/About
menu group. A page with no command (About G'MIC, Privacy Notice, Release Notes, Tribute to
Sébastien) shows its notes and passes Source through; Gmicky Roddy draws the mascots (it
downloads their images). Filters whose commands write files into G'MIC's folders, or at a path
that a command computes, are hidden: they include the pages that download or post (Download
External Data, Send Feedback, What's New?, which would also download the update file), which
keep their data in such files, and Layers To PNG, SVG Symetries A and SVG TSP, which write their
result into G'MIC's folder. This is a rule on the files written, not on network access: the
filters that only download data into G'MIC's cache (Color Presets, Denoise [CNN]...) are shown.

G'MIC's update file is the update of the built-in filters that G'MIC-Qt (when its filter updates
are on) or the command "gmic update" downloads into G'MIC's folder: $GMIC_PATH if set, else
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gmic, else ~/.config/gmic if ~/.config exists, else ~/gmic (Windows:
%APPDATA%/gmic). The plug-in never downloads it, and only reads it as text. It is off by
default: G'MIC-Qt rewrites it on its own (by default, once a month, when it is used), and after
such an update a host's stale plug-in cache may silently make the plug-in's nodes other filters.
With OFX_GMIC_UPDATE_FILE=1, clear the host's plug-in cache after each update. To use the update
of a given day without this risk, copy the file to ~/.config/gmic/gmic_stdlib.gmic instead: the
filters then only change when you copy it again (and clear the cache). COMMERCIAL builds never
read the update file: it has the filters under the GPL, which they leave out.

Preview/Draft Mode (in Advanced Options) renders with the filter's preview command, as G'MIC-Qt's
preview does, instead of its main command. The Preview Type and Preview Split parameters that
many filters have (G'MIC's split preview: the original next to the result) are only used by the
preview command, so they are greyed out unless Preview/Draft Mode is on (not in the filters that
always render with their preview command). To compare the input and the result in Natron, use
the viewer's wipe instead.

Choosing the plug-ins
---------------------

Contents/Resources/gmic_ofx_plugins.txt in the bundle lists every plug-in, one identifier per
line (the identifier that hosts save in projects), grouped by menu. In a Natron install, the
bundle is Plugins/OFX/Natron/GMIC.ofx.bundle (macOS: Natron.app/Contents/Plugins/OFX/Natron).
To disable plug-ins, copy the list to ~/.config/gmic/gmic_ofx_plugins.txt (Windows:
%APPDATA%/gmic/gmic_ofx_plugins.txt) and comment out their lines with '#':

    # --- G'MIC/Degradations ---
    # eu.gmic.AddGrain  # G'MIC/Degradations: G'MIC Add Grain
    eu.gmic.BlurAngular  # G'MIC/Degradations: G'MIC Blur Angular

Here Add Grain is disabled. A line that is only '#' (one or more) and an identifier disables
that plug-in; after an identifier, '#' starts a comment; any other line is a comment. If an
identifier is on several lines, the last one wins. Plug-ins that the file doesn't mention are
enabled, so that the filters that a new version of G'MIC adds appear; an identifier that is no
longer a plug-in (a filter renamed or removed) is ignored. The file may have LF, CRLF or CR line
endings, and be UTF-8, or UTF-16 with a byte-order mark (Windows PowerShell 5's "> file").

When it reads the file, the plug-in prints one line on stderr: how many plug-ins the file
disables, and the disabled identifiers that match no plug-in (typos, or filters that G'MIC
removed). To see it, run the host from a terminal (on Windows, NatronRenderer: Natron.exe has
no console), or use the log file (see "Messages"). A comment line that is a single word after
its '#' (e.g. "#-----") reads as a disabled identifier, and is reported as unknown: put a space
in it ("# -----").

The plug-in reads the file when the host loads it. After a change, restart the host, and clear
its OpenFX plug-in cache: this is required. Natron: Cache > Clear Plug-ins Load Cache, or start
it once with --clear-openfx-cache. A cache made with another list keeps the old menus and numbers
the plug-ins differently: after the first disabled plug-in, a node may silently become another
filter. Other hosts with plug-in caches (Nuke, DaVinci Resolve) need theirs cleared too.

A disabled plug-in is not registered at all (OpenFX has no way to only hide a plug-in from the
menus). Natron then reports that it can't load the plug-in of a project's node, and doesn't
create that node: re-enable the plug-in (and clear the cache) before opening and saving such a
project.

The bundle's list is made from the standard library built into the plug-in: it doesn't follow a
user's gmic_ofx.gmic, G'MIC's update file or ~/.gmic (see "Filters"). For a list of the filters
actually loaded, set OFX_GMIC_WRITE_PLUGIN_LIST (see "Environment").

Points
------

A filter's points (its point() parameters) are handles on the host's viewer, as G'MIC-Qt's
keypoints are on its preview, each in the colour that the filter gives it. Drag a point to move
it: most points render while they are dragged (G'MIC's "burst" points), the others when they are
released. A removable point that is off (its "Enabled" checkbox), or a point that only the
filter's preview uses without Preview/Draft Mode, has no handle. COMMERCIAL builds, and hosts
without overlays, keep the host's own handles, if it has some.

Rendering
---------

Results of another size (issue #175): as in G'MIC-Qt, a filter's result may be larger, smaller or
moved (Rotate, frames, crops, Upscale [Scale2x]..., or a pos(x,y) in the result's name, from
Source's top-left corner). The Resize Mode (in Advanced Options) chooses the output:
- Dynamic, the default: the result's size and position. If some result of the filter's run has a
  non-zero pos(x,y) (a layout: tiles, speech bubbles, layers moved by the filter), each result is
  placed as G'MIC-Qt places a layer, its top-left corner moved by x to the right and y down from
  Source's. Otherwise (no pos, G'MIC's default pos(0,0), or an input's name kept: most filters,
  e.g. Upscale [Scale2x], frames, rotations, montages, and also crops, downscales and tiles
  without a pos, which G'MIC-Qt places at the top-left corner) the result keeps Source's
  bottom-left corner, the origin of OFX images: a 2x upscale of a 1920x1080 Source at 0,0 is at
  0,0-3840,2160, a 50% downscale at 0,0-960,540 (issue #238). OpenFX needs the
  size and position before the render: the plug-in runs the filter on opaque gradient images and
  predicts them:
  - three small probes with Source's long side at 64, 80 and 96 pixels (the inputs' proportions
    kept, about 15k pixels), two thin probes that span the real size, Source's real width x 48
    pixels and 48 pixels x its real height, and a check probe with Source's long side at 256
    pixels: about 195k pixels in all for 1920x1080;
  - a size or position that the small probes give as an exact fraction of the input's (2x, 50%,
    150%, fixed borders), and that also gives the thin and check probes exactly, is exact;
  - any other affine one (e.g. a rotation, 10%) is fitted against both the width and the height
    of the small and thin probes, and must give the check probe within a pixel: the prediction
    is then within a pixel, with a 1-pixel transparent margin on the sides that could be short,
    so that the result fits without a warning;
  - if a fit fails (a size in rounded percent of max(w,h), as some frames, one that isn't an
    affine function of the input's, such as sqrt(W*H) or W*H/1024, or a Source so thin that its
    small probes are 1 pixel high), or a probe fails: one probe at the real size, exact, at the
    cost of a render on a gradient. A Source no larger than the probes would be (e.g. 160x120)
    is probed at its own size.
  The probes run again only after an edit, on every frame when a parameter is animated, or when
  an input's size changes (for Output Layer "Frame", on every frame). A prediction stops after
  10 s in all: the output then keeps Source's size (a note on stderr), and the same values and
  sizes are not tried again for 30 s, so that a slow filter doesn't block the host on every
  query. A prediction that runs out of memory keeps Source's size too, and is tried again at the
  next query. Sizes that depend on the image's content (automatic crops, crops of the
  transparent parts) can't be predicted. A result that doesn't fit the output is cropped or
  padded, with a warning on the node.
- Fixed (Inplace): Source's size and position. A result of another size is placed as with
  Dynamic, then cropped or padded to Source's rectangle, never resized: switching between Fixed
  and Dynamic never moves the content (before #238, Fixed cropped from the top-left corner and
  ignored pos).
- Downsample 1/N: the filter runs on Source downsampled N times, and the result is resized to
  Source's size.
Results of Source's size render as before in every mode. The inputs may have any size and
position (the plug-in supports OpenFX multi-resolution); a layer input elsewhere than Source gets
its offset from Source as its pos(x,y), as in G'MIC-Qt. On hosts without multi-resolution, the
output always has Source's size, and Fixed and Dynamic resize the result to it, as before.

A render can be aborted while G'MIC runs. The filters that report their progress to G'MIC (with
its progress command; most filters don't) show it in the host's progress bar, e.g. Natron's
Progress panel, which lists a render once it is expected to last a few seconds. The plug-in
ignores the panel's Cancel button: abort the render itself (e.g. Natron's viewer or render
stop button).

Render scale (Natron's proxy mode, a zoomed-out viewer): G'MIC filters have no unit for their
sizes, so most of them don't give the same result on a downscaled image. The plug-in follows the
preview factor after the filter's preview command (see the #@gui lines in gmic_stdlib.gmic), as
G'MIC-Qt does for its preview zoom:
- "+" after the factor, e.g. "(0)+" or "(1)+": the preview is right at any zoom. In Natron, these
  filters render at the render scale, on inputs at that scale, with G'MIC-Qt's preview area
  variables at that scale too (_preview_x0 to _preview_y1; _preview_width and _preview_height
  with Preview/Draft Mode), as in G'MIC-Qt's zoomed preview. 63 filters with G'MIC 4.0.8: colour
  mixers, CLUTs, Black & White... "+" is the filter author's claim. Colour-only filters look the
  same at any scale, but the "+" filters with sizes in pixels (Ascii Art, Painting, Hard
  Painting, Local Contrast, Pen Drawing, Denoise Smooth, Sharpen FFT...) look different at a
  lower scale: e.g. Ascii Art's characters are twice as large, relative to the image, at half
  scale. That is also what G'MIC-Qt's zoomed preview shows. Natron renders at a lower scale by
  itself when its viewer is zoomed to 50% or less, not only in proxy mode: the final render, at
  full size, is not affected.
- No factor, a factor alone (G'MIC-Qt only shows the preview at that zoom), or "*" (the preview
  needs the whole image at full size): Natron renders these at full size, then downscales the
  result.
Other hosts don't render at full size instead: there, every filter renders at the render scale,
with sizes in pixels unscaled.

In G'MIC-Qt, Apply follows a preview, and some filters need what their preview command leaves:
G'MIC's _persistent variable, or parameter values that the preview's status sets (e.g. Bit-Plane
Shuffler, Tupper's Self-Referential Formula, Shear). The plug-in renders these the same way: it
runs the filter's preview command first, on the same input, then its main command with the
preview's _persistent and status, so they take up to twice as long. A filter is rendered so if its
main command, or a command that it calls, uses _persistent, or if it has a hidden value()
parameter that the preview's status fills. _persistent starts empty on every render (G'MIC-Qt
keeps it from one run of a filter to the next), so that a render doesn't depend on the previous
ones. With Preview/Draft Mode on, only the preview command runs, as before. When it loads the
filters, the plug-in prints one line for each of these ("openfx-gmic: previewFirst <id>", see
"Messages"). If the preview command fails, the main command runs without its state, and a
warning on the node says so.

G'MIC's button() parameters (e.g. Mandelbrot - Julia Sets' Zoom In, Randomize Seed) are push
buttons. In G'MIC-Qt, a click makes a preview with the button pressed, then sets the parameter
values that the preview's status gives. Here, a press runs the filter's preview command once with
the button pressed, on the input at the current frame scaled down to at most 512 pixels (as
G'MIC-Qt's preview image; the filter gets G'MIC-Qt's _preview_timeout, 16 seconds), and sets those
values (one undo step). The host waits for the run: its progress shows at once, and its Cancel
(e.g. in Natron's Progress panel) stops it, without changing anything. Values that depend on the
image size may differ slightly from those of a full-size run. Renders get 0 for a button, as
G'MIC-Qt's Apply, so a button whose filter sets no values does nothing. One difference: in G'MIC-Qt,
with its Preview off and a filter that sets no status, a click makes no run, and the next Apply
gets 1 for the button. Buttons were checkboxes before: Natron doesn't load such a saved checkbox
(another parameter type), and the other saved values are unchanged; a script can't set them
either (a push button has no value).

Many filters change their own parameters as you use them (e.g. Transfer Colors [Multi] shows Source
Color 1 to 8 only when Color Correspondences is above 0): after each preview, G'MIC-Qt applies the
status that the filter's preview command sets, which gives parameter values, and shows, hides or
disables parameters. Here, for the filters whose preview command sets a status, the plug-in runs
that command after each edit of the filter's parameters (once per change that the host reports),
and when Source is connected, the same way as a press (512 pixels, 16 seconds, Cancel), and sets
the values that the status gives (not an undo step of its own: undoing the edit runs the status
again). On Natron only, it also shows, hides and disables the parameters as the status says, and
they start as in G'MIC-Qt; Natron saves this state in projects. In a project saved with an older
version of the plug-in, which showed every parameter of these filters, the parameters left at their
default value start in G'MIC-Qt's initial state (some hidden or disabled), the others shown: edit
any parameter of the node to update them. The visibilities follow the values at the time of the
edit: moving to another frame of an animation doesn't update them. Other hosts show and enable
every parameter of these filters. Points and their checkboxes always stay visible, since a host draws no
handle for a hidden or disabled parameter. The run makes the host wait after each edit, more so with
a large input or a slow filter.

Environment
-----------

- OFX_GMIC_UPDATE_FILE: 1 makes the plug-in read G'MIC's filter update file, update408.gmic, if
  no user filter file comes first (see "Filters"); unset (the default) or any other value (with a
  note on stderr): the file is ignored. After G'MIC-Qt updates the file, clear the host's plug-in cache. Ignored by
  COMMERCIAL builds.
- OFX_GMIC_MULTIRES: a test aid. 0: the plug-in acts as on a host without multi-resolution support
  (Resize Mode Fixed and Dynamic resize the result to Source's size, see "Rendering"), with a note
  on stderr if the host has it; the CI tests set it on openfx's hostDemo to cover those hosts.
  Unset, the host's own property decides; any other value is ignored, with a note on stderr.
- OFX_GMIC_FRAME_CACHE_MB: memory budget, in MB, of the cache of Output Layer "Frame" results
  (default: 1/8 of the physical memory, between 1 and 4 GiB; 0 disables the cache).
  A decimal number >= 0 ("512", "0.5", "2e3"; '.' as decimal point whatever the locale,
  surrounding whitespace allowed): a sign, inf, nan, hexadecimal, other text or an overflow give
  the default, with a note on stderr.
- OMP_NUM_THREADS=<n>: a fixed number of OpenMP threads, for each render and for the whole host
  process (other OpenMP code in it too): the plug-in then leaves G'MIC's thread count alone.
  OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 makes G'MIC's OpenMP loops single-threaded (its parallel command still
  starts its own threads). Unset (the default), each render uses at least the number of hardware
  threads divided by the G'MIC renders running at once in the process, or more if the host's
  multithread suite gives more (Natron: the threads that its other renders leave free; the floor
  overrides its "render threads -1" and "threads per effect" settings, which G'MIC ignored
  before too). A host whose suite always reports 1 thus runs a single render with all the
  hardware threads, and several renders with their share each. A filter that uses G'MIC's
  parallel command goes back to all the CPUs after it. Filters with random numbers (Global
  Random Seed) give results that depend on the number of threads: set OMP_NUM_THREADS for
  results that don't change with the renders running at once.
- OFX_GMIC_WRITE_PLUGIN_LIST=<file>: the plug-in writes its list of the plug-ins (see "Choosing
  the plug-ins") to <file> when the host first loads it, from the filters it loaded (e.g. a
  gmic_ofx.gmic), those that the user's list disables commented out. Every process that loads
  the plug-in with it set writes the same file (e.g. a host's render processes): set it for one
  run only. The build makes the bundle's list so.

Messages
--------

The plug-in prints its messages (when it loads the filters, and some notes while rendering) on
stderr, as G'MIC does with the messages of the Log Verbosity parameter (Level 1 to 3; Off, the
default, prints none; Level 3 is G'MIC-Qt's "very verbose" level, G'MIC's `v 3`). A host
started from the Dock, the Start menu or a desktop launcher shows no stderr: to keep them, create
an empty file gmic_ofx_log in ~/.config/gmic (Windows:
%APPDATA%/gmic), as G'MIC-Qt's "log file" output messages do with gmic_qt_log. Every host
process that loads the plug-in then appends its messages to that file instead of stderr, from
the first host start after the file exists; delete it to go back to stderr (on Windows, quit the
host first: the file can't be deleted while a host has it open). Hosts running at the same time
(e.g. Natron and its render processes) write to the same file, and their lines may be
interleaved. G'MIC-Qt empties its log at each start; the plug-in keeps the previous messages,
and only empties the file when a host starts with it larger than 16 MB. The build, which writes
the bundle's plug-in list (OFX_GMIC_WRITE_PLUGIN_LIST), keeps its messages on stderr.

After a successful render, a warning on the node (Natron: its persistent message) tells when
Output Layer "Frame" runs the filter on every frame, because its results are too many or too
large to keep, or one of them is empty, and when a result doesn't fit the output that Resize Mode
Dynamic declared (it is then cropped or padded). G'MIC errors stay error messages on the node.

Tests
-----

There are no unit tests. The CI scripts can be run locally:
- .github/workflows/describe_plugins.sh <dir with GMIC.ofx.bundle> <plug-in count>
  builds a minimal OpenFX host and describes every plug-in; the built-in standard library must
  give exactly that number of plug-ins (GMIC_PLUGIN_COUNT in ci.yml).
- .github/workflows/render_plugins.py renders the plug-ins with openfx's hostDemo (CMake with
  -DGMIC_BUILD_TESTS=ON); see the script's help.

Contact
-------

Report bugs at https://github.com/NatronGitHub/openfx-gmic/issues. Bugs in the filters
themselves belong upstream: https://github.com/GreycLab/gmic (G'MIC and its standard library)
or https://github.com/GreycLab/gmic-community (community filters).
